WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Selling concert tickets is… not especially hard.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.500
Despite what your $20 “service fee”&nbsp;
would suggest, on a scale of difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.080
problems to solve, it lies somewhere between&nbsp;
light bulb installation and check cashing.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:23.460
The only remotely hard part — you might say&nbsp;
a ticketing company’s one job — is to handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.140
the extremely predictable surge of traffic&nbsp;
the day Taylor Swift tickets go on sale.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.660
So, of course, that’s exactly what TicketMaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.220
failed spectacularly at during&nbsp;
her recent “Eras” tour presale.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:47.160
Traumatized fans told stories of $200 service&nbsp;
fees, cryptic error messages, and $50,000 seats.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.160
Most left with nothing to show for their&nbsp;
eight hours of fighting in the trenches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.140
Others felt like lottery winners&nbsp;
simply for having been granted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.420
the privilege of paying five, six, or&nbsp;
nine hundred dollars for nosebleeds.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.520
But although demand for this tour&nbsp;
could hardly have been higher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.120
there’s nothing new about the&nbsp;
unpleasantness of buying tickets.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.100
When service fees commonly&nbsp;
cost more than the actual seat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:22.620
it’s no surprise America’s hatred of&nbsp;
TicketMaster makes Comcast look favorable.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.280
How does the company get away with it?

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.660
“Because,” common sense would say, “they can.”

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:38.940
TicketMaster is so deeply embedded in the industry&nbsp;
that it’s nearly impossible for large artists to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:47.820
avoid. Of Taylor Swift’s 52 upcoming shows,&nbsp;
for instance, it sold the tickets for 47.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.640
Not only does it sell twice as many&nbsp;
tickets as its closest competitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:00.120
StubHub, but it also represents performers,&nbsp;
owns venues, and acts as a promoter.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.620
All of this is 100% true.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.840
And yet, it’s not the reason&nbsp;
service fees are astronomical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.940
scalping is rampant, or buying&nbsp;
tickets, just generally sucks.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.820
Sponsored by Audible. Listen to “Ticket Masters:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.300
The Rise of the Concert Industry&nbsp;
and How the Public Got Scalped”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.220
or one of thousands of other great audiobooks&nbsp;
for free with the link in the description.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.020
What’s the “right” price for a product?

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.100
Ask the seller and they’ll&nbsp;
tell you “that’s easy!”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.520
the right price is the one&nbsp;
people are willing to pay.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.020
Entire careers and disciplines exist for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.380
sole purpose of finding that&nbsp;
number with penny precision.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:56.580
And because price has the largest impact&nbsp;
on profit — more so than production costs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:02.040
or even volume sold — the science of&nbsp;
pricing has gotten pretty darn good.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:09.240
Through surveys, trends, and algorithms,&nbsp;
companies systematically deconstruct the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:16.020
value consumers place on each feature.&nbsp;
A $3 gallon of milk might derive 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:20.940
cents of its value from being reduced&nbsp;
fat and a nickel from its packaging.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.920
They can then use those values to&nbsp;
determine the theoretical price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.960
of a new product or a matrix of products&nbsp;
with different combinations of features.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.840
Concerts, on the other hand, are&nbsp;
just about the opposite of milk.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.840
Not only are they an experience but&nbsp;
they’re also highly emotionally charged.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.560
Besides, no two shows are the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:53.940
Taylor Swift will only ever play the Eras&nbsp;
Tour in Kansas City on July 7th once.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.380
Now, none of this would faze an economist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:05.640
After all, there are many one-of-a-kind products&nbsp;
— houses, paintings, and used cars, for example.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.020
When you don’t know the&nbsp;
consumer’s willingness to pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:15.480
the simple “Econ 101” solution&nbsp;
is to force them to reveal it.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.120
In other words, an auction.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.220
All the technology required&nbsp;
to double or triple Taylor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:28.080
Swift’s profit overnight existed on eBay in 1995.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.500
The problem, of course, is that artists are&nbsp;
in the business of cultivating goodwill.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:40.200
These higher prices would effectively&nbsp;
exclude many of their biggest fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.880
bruising their image, and limiting&nbsp;
the long-term growth of their fanbase.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.360
Take a second to appreciate&nbsp;
what this means: Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.780
artists artificially price their tickets hundreds&nbsp;
or even thousands of dollars below market value.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.640
We know this because the same&nbsp;
tickets are immediately resold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.160
for many multiples of their original price.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:09.540
Remember, from a purely economic perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.840
the “right” price is the one&nbsp;
people are willing to pay.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:22.740
And a natural consequence is the opportunity for&nbsp;
scalpers to buy low and sell high. If Taylor Swift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:29.580
won’t charge what she’s worth, some random dude&nbsp;
in Romania is more than happy to do it for her.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.700
At the same time, artists are also&nbsp;
in the business of… making money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.720
They still want to capture&nbsp;
some of that extra value.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.940
And one of the ways they balance&nbsp;
these two competing interests is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.180
by secretly withholding many of their best seats.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:55.020
Some of these “holds” may be given to&nbsp;
friends and family. Others, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.020 --> 00:06:01.200
are sold directly to brokers who turn around and&nbsp;
sell them to you at the prevailing market rate.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.420
In 2011, Katy Petty’s contract&nbsp;
was leaked to the public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.740
revealing that she, like many&nbsp;
musicians, did just that.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:17.520
In other words, artists get to have their&nbsp;
cake and eat it too — protecting their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:24.120
image with affordable primary ticket sales while&nbsp;
profiting off far more expensive secondary sales.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.300
Now, you might argue that artists&nbsp;
themselves are victims of the larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.440
industry — one they have no&nbsp;
choice but to participate in.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:36.720
This is true.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:42.960
Unless Taylor Swift wants to play for crowds&nbsp;
of 800 people at the Timbuktu county fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.980
TicketMaster is effectively the only game in town.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.500
That’s because it signs 3-5&nbsp;
year exclusive contracts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.860
the vast majority of large, desirable venues.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.000
All the star power of Sinatra, Elvis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.620
and Lennon combined would be worthless&nbsp;
against these air-tight contracts.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.020
If you want to play at these venues —&nbsp;
which, for the most popular artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.640
really means play at all —&nbsp;
TicketMaster comes with them.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:22.380
But what critics of the company often miss by&nbsp;
fixating on its monopoly is the question why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.640
Why do companies sign these&nbsp;
exclusive deals with TicketMaster?

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:34.080
Once upon a time, ticketing was a cost&nbsp;
for venues — a service, like security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.540
that they outsourced to the lowest bidder.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:42.480
What TicketMaster did — it’s innovation,&nbsp;
if you can call it that — was approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:49.560
these venues with an offer: “What if,&nbsp;
instead of you paying us, we paid you?”

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:56.340
Sure, part of the reason TicketMaster got&nbsp;
so big was by swallowing up its competitors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:01.620
but it also did so by promising&nbsp;
to make concert venues more money.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:09.000
For cash-strapped public stadiums especially, this&nbsp;
guaranteed source of revenue proved irresistible.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:16.320
It did this with the two most hated words&nbsp;
in the English language: service fees.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.240
Every show is different, but these&nbsp;
fees are typically shared between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.160
some combination of TicketMaster,&nbsp;
the venue, promoter, and/or artist.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:33.000
The truth — though it pains me to say this —&nbsp;
is that the vast majority of TicketMaster’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:40.560
customers love it. It’s just that we are&nbsp;
not its customers. We are just collateral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.540
damage caught in a web of intermediaries&nbsp;
fighting to capture the value of live music.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.720
That’s what the Justice Department&nbsp;
decided in 1995 after a years-long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:57.120
investigation — that its true customers&nbsp;
were perfectly happy with the status quo.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.180
A decade later, a Federal District Court&nbsp;
in California reached the same conclusion.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.240
There’s no doubt TicketMaster&nbsp;
wields far more control than any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.880
one company should have over the music industry.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:16.200
In fact, since it merged with Live Nation in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:21.000
TicketMaster itself owns many of the&nbsp;
venues that grant it exclusivity.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.420
These things are easy to understand.

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:30.180
What’s not as well understood is&nbsp;
that greater competition isn’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.460
in this case, an effective solution.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:39.480
Remember: ticketing companies compete to give&nbsp;
more money to venues in the form of service fees.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:45.480
Split TicketMaster in two and they&nbsp;
would bid service fees up, not down.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:51.900
Yes, the company’s vertical integration&nbsp;
— spanning venues, promotion, ticketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:57.540
and managing — is incredibly concerning.&nbsp;
But let’s not forget that it had no trouble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:03.300
securing these exclusive contracts well&nbsp;
before it merged with Live Nation in 2010.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:11.640
In 1994, for instance, it had exclusive deals&nbsp;
with 63% of American arenas and concert halls.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.920
Now, if any of this surprised&nbsp;
you — that’s the point.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:21.960
If you assumed service fees were&nbsp;
entirely the fault of TicketMaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:28.860
you are proof that the company doesn’t just&nbsp;
skim profit, it also offers a valuable service.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:34.200
What TicketMaster is is a reputational shield.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:40.680
Ticketing is the ugly part of the business&nbsp;
— the business part of the business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.620
So, artists, who don’t want to touch&nbsp;
it with a ten-foot pole, outsource it.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:54.120
The implicit agreement is that, while fees get&nbsp;
spread around, TicketMaster, alone, takes the hit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.700
And when fans can’t get seats, it’s&nbsp;
TicketMaster who delivers the bad news.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:05.520
But the critical thing to not lose&nbsp;
sight of is that above all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:11.220
intermediaries is the only absolute,&nbsp;
unbreakable monopoly: the artist.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:16.800
Though he may be on all our&nbsp;
iPhones, there’s only one Bono.

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:22.860
The top 1% of artists negotiate from&nbsp;
a position of extraordinary strength.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:30.360
This may sound like an obvious truism — “the&nbsp;
most popular artists are powerful… duh!” — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:34.860
it’s something fans, in their eagerness&nbsp;
to forgive their heroes, often forget.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:39.060
Consider, for instance, how they get paid.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.240
Artists, through their agents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.620
who typically take a percentage of their&nbsp;
earnings, are approached by a promoter.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:52.860
Promoters are the heart of the whole&nbsp;
operation. Beyond managing, scheduling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:58.380
and, yes, promoting, by far their most&nbsp;
important role is to underwrite the event.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:04.440
While artists receive a guarantee — an&nbsp;
up-front fee for their performance — say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:09.480
a million dollars, the promoter may&nbsp;
make more than a million dollars or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:13.380
they might make far less — they assume that risk.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:18.360
The most popular musicians —&nbsp;
confident in their ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:22.440
attract crowds — often choose a&nbsp;
percentage of the door instead.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:29.340
And the higher their guarantee or percentage,&nbsp;
the less money is left over for everyone else.

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:35.580
When top artists can demand as much as&nbsp;
one hundred and five percent — more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:40.440
the total ticket sales —it’s no wonder&nbsp;
these costs eventually ripple down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:47.160
you and I in the form of $12 pretzels and $50&nbsp;
service fees — it had to come from somewhere.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.940
And that’s just one part of the contract.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:57.000
Musicians also help determine ticket&nbsp;
prices, fees, presale requirements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:02.040
and whether or not to use dynamic pricing&nbsp;
— which adjusts prices algorithmically.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:08.160
This is not at all to paint artists&nbsp;
as the villains and TicketMaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:12.780
as an innocent scapegoat. They’re&nbsp;
all complicit to varying degrees.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.600
But if your goal is to affect change,&nbsp;
calling for the breakup of TicketMaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:24.060
is more a satisfying method of venting&nbsp;
frustration than it is an effective solution.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:28.500
The real hope lies with the artist — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:33.000
party both with the most power and&nbsp;
the one most responsive to fans.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:38.100
It may not be the musician's fault&nbsp;
that the industry is what it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:43.860
but if anyone can negotiate on behalf&nbsp;
of all of us, it’s Taylor Swift, who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:48.240
in 2015, almost single-handedly&nbsp;
changed how artists are paid on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:52.740
Apple Music and more recently fought&nbsp;
back against her own record label.

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:59.280
Just as the costs imposed by the top 1%&nbsp;
of artists ripple down to the rest of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:03.720
these privileged few can also set&nbsp;
better terms that benefit everyone.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:10.440
Music history is filled with exactly&nbsp;
that — bands standing up for their fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.320
sometimes to borderline comical degrees.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:19.620
When the jam band “The String Cheese Incident”&nbsp;
was refused the right to sell its own tickets in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:24.240
the early 2000s, it threatened to hand&nbsp;
out cash to thousands of volunteers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:30.000
who would go buy them on their behalf at the&nbsp;
box office, which was exempt from service fees.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:36.840
The Grateful Dead, likewise, fought TicketMaster&nbsp;
for the right to sell half of its tickets directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:42.420
to fans. In order to prevent scalpers from&nbsp;
buying more than the permitted number of tickets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:47.520
it would spread thousands of order slips out on&nbsp;
the floor and look for duplicate handwriting.

